//
// decode_geometry.py's self-check, as a PR gate.
//
// decode_geometry derives every host BO size the decode sweep dispatches with
// by importing the builder (fused_decode.py, or the fused_decode_ple fork for
// gemma4-e2b) and reading its constants. That coupling is deliberate -- the
// alternative is restating the builder's arithmetic and letting the two drift
// -- but it means an ordinary edit to the builder can break the sweep for every
// model without touching a line the sweep owns.
//
// Which is what happened. #1989 dropped MULTIBLK, a builder constant pinned to
// True, and decode_geometry was its last reader; ten of the eleven sweeps died
// on AttributeError before any NPU dispatch. Only gemma4-e2b survived, because
// it imports the PLE fork where the constant still exists, so the breakage read
// as one model's problem when it was every other model's. Nothing ran --check,
// so a two-second pure-Python failure took two ~4.5h nightlies to surface.
//
// --check is a value gate, not a smoke test: it re-derives w_elems, the
// qwen3-8b / llama-3.1-8b weight splits and the rope-per-layer geometry against
// expected numbers carried in the script, and reproduces bench_decode.cpp's
// built-in defaults for llama-3.2-1b. A change that keeps the import working
// but shifts a size still fails here.
//
// REQUIRES is deliberately empty. This needs no NPU, no xclbin, no HF weights
// and no secrets -- it imports Python modules and does arithmetic. Gating it on
// ryzen_ai_npu2 or peano the way the model lits do would make it silently
// unsupported on exactly the runners where a cheap gate is most useful. The
// builder import resolves through the PYTHONPATH lit already sets (air, aie,
// xrt), so the only requirement is a configured build.
//
// Collected by check-programming-examples-llms-geometry via path-based
// --filter; see programming_examples/CMakeLists.txt.
//
// RUN: %PYTHON %S/decode_geometry.py --check | FileCheck %s
// CHECK: SELF-CHECK PASS
